#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "5d2c0fff-c9af-5e8a-846a-640878f08283" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-730" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = \\ln(x^2-4)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = \\ln u, \\qquad u = x^2-4\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{u} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 4} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x}{x^{2} - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7df7fed3-c172-5cc2-aca8-abd31a0902a0" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-731" :question/problem "If $y = \\log_2(\\ln{x})$, what is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_2 u, \\qquad u = \\ln x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u\\ln 2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{u\\ln 2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{xu\\ln 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x\\ln x\\ln 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e41065-6d6b-538a-ad1b-fb38e2eee773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8ln⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245686} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b4388f-43b9-5120-8829-4b2ac903498c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245687} {:answer/id #uuid "0e9251de-e419-5ed8-ba64-a036e960f360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245688} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5ffcab-1f74-5214-85ea-a1b0c723d37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{ln⁡8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245689} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8a9a64-8ad0-5872-84f5-3dbf2a524064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2b4388f-43b9-5120-8829-4b2ac903498c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245687} :answer-field/id #uuid "748d2ad4-fbef-5ce7-b05a-18ced597c487" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245685}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69a22f33-b1f5-5ce0-9f9a-c8a43f68878e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-11930" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡(8x)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here $f(x)=ln⁡(8x)$ is a function of a function. We use the chain rule.First, we express $g(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8x}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}}{\\cancel{8}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cefcefd-ab4a-54a9-96ec-7fd9e98ed060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245693} {:answer/id #uuid "58f78de1-bf51-5725-9729-7397ef887e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cefcefd-ab4a-54a9-96ec-7fd9e98ed060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245693} :answer-field/id #uuid "422c0e8e-ef2d-54e8-8efc-86f338261860" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e3a8905-5d05-5bb6-8944-85d54da6c418" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-310144" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(5{x}^{2}+4)$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=5{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & ={\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(1)}{5({1}^{2})+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a389446e-6169-5e82-8df7-14b55703d5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a389446e-6169-5e82-8df7-14b55703d5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245697} :answer-field/id #uuid "fedb2062-2878-5cb4-970d-33e6a45be825" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245696}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf7bff88-8076-590c-9d6c-accf8b15a565" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-310145" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={log}_{4}⁡(6x-2)+7$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}⁡(u)+7,\\,u=6x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}{|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3(1)-1)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76e7711b-030e-5e3e-bceb-b26a6febfd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76e7711b-030e-5e3e-bceb-b26a6febfd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245700} :answer-field/id #uuid "63ce6489-6b9c-5c3e-8c95-d580615b8b68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245699}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06e078d9-14de-5fbb-a951-fa0bc181f2a8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-341671" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡(9x)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9x}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{9}}{\\cancel{9}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cecb1c6-3a1b-5e2f-a84b-92cc6c13b85d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245703} {:answer/id #uuid "f51a9b49-6cac-5136-93b7-a9e6cef0db65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51a9b49-6cac-5136-93b7-a9e6cef0db65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245704} :answer-field/id #uuid "c391a1e2-4765-5821-997f-8890f733fbdd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245702}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bdbe537-8874-525b-aec8-402f441a2044" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-341679" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(7-4x)$ at the point where $x=-1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=7-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7-4x}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=-1} & ={-\\frac{4}{7-4x}|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7-4(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{11}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dafbfec-c1a7-5ce8-84d6-4984fb3f1394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245707} {:answer/id #uuid "7146ea97-87ff-56d7-94f0-6daf91862074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dafbfec-c1a7-5ce8-84d6-4984fb3f1394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245707} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf1029b5-4090-506c-9b0b-049fd97904d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245706}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9016a16d-888e-5ccf-8278-30b6de2dd539" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-341691" :question/problem "Given that $y={log}_{6}⁡(-3{x}^{3}),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{6}⁡(u),\\,u=-3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(6)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(6)}\\cdot (-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-9{x}^{2}}{-3{x}^{3}ln⁡(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot 3}{\\cancel{-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot xln⁡(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{xln⁡(6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3f8ace2-1ea3-59ae-82b9-4f0fb69f53bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245711} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca13ef7-7e2d-52ca-8ce1-bed749495ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245712} {:answer/id #uuid "64df5e4a-30c8-5cf7-850a-04a6baae6127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3xln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245713} {:answer/id #uuid "3850d484-510d-5ff6-baf4-88ba3a43bd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{xln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245714} {:answer/id #uuid "52fb39a7-10aa-5955-9b5d-6a43baf1cabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3850d484-510d-5ff6-baf4-88ba3a43bd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{xln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245714} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e4490d-fdde-5ce7-aa07-9123758e9284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245710}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d872aca0-9860-5508-b389-44ecee8a1a35" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48559" :question/problem "Given that $y={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3}),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{2}⁡(u),\\,u={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(2)}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}ln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot xln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{xln⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afe095d-9ec2-5c11-b7ea-db9e85245279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245718} {:answer/id #uuid "931a5bef-9c6a-582a-bcc7-c4d22079bfbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-8+4ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245719} {:answer/id #uuid "888b24f5-922e-5da1-9fe7-915713648e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245720} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9a34a7-e7c8-51bc-abf6-fa620ea60b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-2-ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245721} {:answer/id #uuid "72b54718-47fa-5ecf-b59c-ff07fb52defb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4+4ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72b54718-47fa-5ecf-b59c-ff07fb52defb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4+4ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245722} :answer-field/id #uuid "974c0400-857b-5e50-86e0-c7b746a19e63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245717}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f656447-8d86-5249-b18f-c8a1f82e6f4b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48654" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=4ln⁡(2x)$ at the point $(4,4ln⁡8).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=4ln⁡u,\\,u=2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{4}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2x}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=4$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={\\frac{4}{x}|}_{x=4}=\\frac{4}{4}=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(4,4ln⁡8)$ is $m=1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4ln⁡(8) & =1(x-4) \\\\ y & =x-4+4ln⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf9fc477-708c-5f22-bf99-c6cfa9d179d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245725} {:answer/id #uuid "538c641b-ef04-5b24-91f4-4bdb3aae5979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245726} {:answer/id #uuid "c544ccdd-2aaf-5acd-a2ee-d0ef9deba832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245727} {:answer/id #uuid "75b89287-4684-5719-bd1e-e17a7b3b5803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245728} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ef1960-6b71-5262-95bd-60b544bab73e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ef1960-6b71-5262-95bd-60b544bab73e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245729} :answer-field/id #uuid "b98b6051-6b87-5677-8d65-c3dac87def85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245724}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d30a7821-a43d-5a20-b43b-a88de1758226" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48656" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={log}_{3}⁡(5x-2)+5$ at the point where $x=\\frac{7}{15}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u)+5,\\,u=5x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=\\frac{7}{15}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=7/15} & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}{|}_{x=7/15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5(\\frac{7}{15})-2)ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184df436-4da1-5c0f-96a3-0e3deafe1be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245732} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d0ce7b-c8ae-5ca5-b0cc-3e7afa91688c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245733} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1c4470-59a5-56a6-b16d-9eb3c32646be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245734} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ad5c5-0f08-555e-bc16-9c770278830b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245735} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfa62c2-8a78-5aed-bd86-2fbb168fcce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ad5c5-0f08-555e-bc16-9c770278830b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245735} :answer-field/id #uuid "f51f6fbd-5372-5bf4-8e80-40eb6da4aff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245731}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "322487ee-970a-5964-8456-5a0db7eac8ba" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-71478" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{2x}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u),\\,u=\\sqrt{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}ln⁡(3)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
